    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95638.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "Well, you, I think first of all, you have to understand the nature of Congress. So basically you have in the House of Representatives, you have people who are elected for two-year terms. And so the representatives come and go. Now, I know that's not the popular perception, but the representatives are a part of the non-permanent representation. What becomes permanent is the staff. And so you have staffers that go from office to office to office to office as the members of Congress change. And that's the situation, basically, that exists on Capitol Hill. And then over on the Senate side, although I never served in the Senate, the terms are much longer, so you have a six-year term, but then What is required for you to remain there is such that your attention can be drawn toward something else. And we'll talk about that something and also a little bit later as well. So basically what you have is the staff on the house and the senate sites. And they basically run the show. So if you have influence over a staffer, that influence that extends to the member of Congress. So basically placement of staff in congressional offices becomes important. And then relationships with staff in congressional offices become important. And so now you see as the newbie going in, I wasn't aware of any of this, right? And because of, I guess my nature, I love to do research, I love to",
