    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95638.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "when we had the, like, for example, the talking points generally come from staff. But in this particular instance of 911, I can't say where they came from, but they all basically said the same thing. We were hit because we were free. And that was my basic objection, because I understand that the US taxpayers have spent trillions of dollars for a military and an intelligence infrastructure, and it failed four times in one day. And I need Because, you know, my father was in the Georgia legislature. I was also in the Georgia legislature with my dad, which was kind of unique. But the one thing my father always told me was that taxpayers' money is sacrosanct. And so, therefore, you don't vote for tax increases. You don't do this. You don't do that without a solid rationale. Absolutely. Yes. And then you also don't vote spending bills without a solid rationale. And so when you take the inventory, say for example, the Pentagon itself that is designed for a power projection around the world, and it can't even defend its headquarters You've got a problem, Houston, we've got a problem, you know? This is, I mean, when you look at the series of events that took place on September 11, it is clear for any critical thinking person, let alone member of Congress, that there were tremendous failures that needed to be investigated. And so basically what started my travails was I understood that national transportation safety board kicks into action whenever there's a plane crash, a train derailment, any kind of. And so there's these independent investigations, national transportation safety board is independent of the Democrats and the Republicans, purely professional going there. And with the power that they have, are able to make design changes to force design changes when they find flaws in design. And so, I mean, you know, there's, there was supposed to have been some kind of, design analysis of what happened on September 11, so that the design of the system could be perfected to make sure that that didn't happen again. And I remember women were interviewed in the president, oh, we don't want this to happen again. Okay, no, we don't want it to happen again. So what was the flaw in the system that allowed this to happen? That was never given to us. That information was never given to us. And so instead, we were supposed to be satisfied with we were hit because we were free. I was not sad.",
