    "turn_text": "That's documented. Yes. And so that therein lies my point of departure. So, but you have to understand that I came into the Congress with certain information anyway. I didn't call it the deep state at that time, but you have to understand my own background and my own interests that I brought with me to the Congress. And this is what I used to say all of the time. When I was studying international relations in grad school, I would say that the the government is comprised of individuals who bring with them into the government their own perceptual screen. So I had my own perceptual screen that I had taken to Washington DC. That screen included knowledge of Co-and-Tell Pro, the Counterintelligence Program, of which I had become quite a student. And that in and of itself has some history, because my father was one of the first, well, he was one of the first black police officers in the city of Atlanta, which brought problems on our family anyway, but then he was politically active in the civil rights movement, which compounded those problems. And so I understood surveillance, you know, because That kind of thing was happened to us. I understood political targeting for elimination from political office, from public office. I understood all of that because it was happening all around me to all of my father's friends. And so I brought that in. And then I had also this knowledge of my undergraduate studies were in international relations, my graduate school, the Fletcher School of Law and Diplomacy. There I studied international relations, got my masters in international relations. And then I read the book by John Stockwell in search of enemies. I read all of this prior to my actually joining the Congress. And so I had these very powerful lessons about what the United States government really was, not what it is portrayed to be or how it defines itself, but I knew from hard experience what the United States really was. I was exposed to that soft underbelly of gritter crime corruption. You know, I got that. And so that awareness then led me onto the International Relations Committee. I was outraged at U.S. foreign policy. and onto the agricultural agriculture committee because my district, first district, they redistricted me literally every two years to try and get me out.",
