  {
    "episode_id": 95638,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95638.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 66,
    "turn_text": "Kind of thing, you know, because I was completely, my questions were completely out of the box and informed. For example, we've got the recent news that President Trump used the brother of all bombs, the Moab bomb, against training camps in Afghanistan. Well, I just happened to have been aware of the fact that this is going way, way, way back. I can't even remember the year now. When, oh, I guess this was after September 11th, and there's all this information coming out about, oh, I'm up in God, oh, I'm up in God, oh, I'm up in God, you better be out praying. Okay. So then the, members of Congress, the House International Relations Committee is having State Department experts come and talk to us. And so they basically are, you know, going on this Osama bin Laden thing and we've got to be afraid of him. And one of my colleagues then asked a question about, well, what's the relationship between the US government and Osama bin Laden. And he kind of fluffed it off. And then I asked, would the gentle woman yield? And so before her time was up, she yielded to me. And so then I got my time in her time. And I said, is it not true? that the United States government spent hundreds of millions of dollars building tunnels in Afghanistan and those, that complex, that training complex was built by the Bin Laden family. And so then what totally changed was he knew he couldn't fluff me off with his response. And then he came back, you can search the congressional record on this. And then he came back and said, well, that money was spent for construction. It was spent with the family,",
