    "turn_idx": 116,
    "turn_text": "And you've got people who are the victims of an unjust system. And they're locked away. Because quite frankly, they're inconvenient. They are, you know, their anger is misplaced. And so instead of striking out at a system that is unjust, they strike out in each other. And therein lies basically the substance of my message that has been for quite a while, because as a southerner, I have seen transformation. I just posted on my Facebook page, which is Cynthia McKinney, official, I just posted on my Facebook page, hey, I remember that I was there. In Decatur, Alabama, it was the first time I saw the Klan. They were all dressed up in their regalia. They had rifles. The National Guard had been called out. And we were protesting to save the life of a young man who was mentally challenged, had been accused of raping a white woman, you know, typical charge. And so, but, you know, they wanted to kill it. And so we were saying, of course, against the death penalty. So saying no less, you know, have some compassion here and understand what might actually the totality of the circumstances that might have happened. So anyway, the attitudes I just posted it because, you know, where we came from as a country and particularly in the South. That's why, you know, when I find a good southern white man that's been through redemption, you can't beat them. I mean, you know, in terms of values and principles and Having done taking the time to be introspective and look and say, okay, let me look at my role in this and let me see where I fit in.",
