    "turn_idx": 118,
    "turn_text": "what I can do to transform myself as well as a system. And so I saw that in operation when the the the powers that be went after john at works, of course, he made it easy for them to do so. But this southern man who's been through that rough rough rough time. and says, okay, comes out on the other side and says, look, we've got to do better as a country. His message is no different than that of Bobby Kennedy. His promise was no different than that of JFK. And, you know, his fallibility was the fallibility of most men. And so his whole political career was derailed. But the point is that I've been up close with members of the militia, Christian identity, Ku Klux Klan members. When I first ran for Congress in 1992, it was the first time I'd even been inside rural Georgia. And in some of the towns I campaigned in, actually, you can see the story was done. I think Time Magazine or somebody came down and did a story of people refusing to shake my hand because of the racial polarization. And I was told, I hit one town and I was told, you better not be here, come sundown. So this is 1992. This is not 1992. 52. This is 1992 that I was told get out of town before the sun goes down. And so after I won the congressional seat in what basically was labeled a gerrymandered district to empower black people to rule over white people, basically what I did was I said, okay, we're going to have unity meetings. And I learned a lot in the process of those unity meetings, but I remember I'll never forget I had one mayor of a small town whisper in my ear, Cynthia, you really did it, because even the Klan is here. And that was my purpose was OK, we do have something called the 1964 Civil Rights Act. We do have something called the 1965 Voting Rights Act. We do have something called the 1968 Fair Housing Act. It is not 1950s. It is the 1990s. And so, therefore, we're going to behave as if it's 1990. One thing I went into every looking cranny of the district after I won, and there were some districts like you have, I mean, some counties in Georgia, you have to understand with the racial polarization the way it is, some counties totally, totally, totally white. No black people, it's like no black people are loud, so no black people go there. And I went to those counties as the Congresswoman goes on with the protection. And they said it's the first time anybody ever came to us. And so then what I learned was that whites in the US thought they had representation, but they only had a representative just like black people did. And in the form of governance that we have, a government that we have, people strive politically for representation, not just to have a representative. And so what I tried to do was to bring representation to every voice in my district. And so I was given the opportunity to act. They wouldn't come to me. It was like a boycott. So like, for example, one of the first things I did was I organized farmers, because we were in the middle of a drought in Georgia. So I organized my farmers to go up to Washington and plead their case for drought relief and extra assistance so that we wouldn't suffer so much. In many respects because of course, you know the food prices go up and all this sort of thing. So you want to have your food supply protected at all costs and of the white farmers came. Not a single white farmer from my district came. I had two white farmers to show up because they heard about it, but they were in another district. So I had two. I had a busload of farmers who did go. They were all black and none of the white farmers except the two. We went to Washington, did everything, was so successful. Then after we were so successful, then white farmers called and called me to come. And they said, I asked them, I said, how come you didn't come? And they said, Cynthia, we just didn't know. They didn't know that I would represent them, just like I would represent anybody else. So, you know, there's lots of, I've got lots of experiences and lots of stories of transformation and belief that people are good. And I do believe, I firmly believe that we can find the good in people. All we have to do is just be open and allow it to",
